WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.080
- Hi everyone.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.350
Good evening and welcome to our

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.500
Franke Program, Mapping
as Knowing Lecture Series.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.390
We have a wonderful talk this evening.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.190
I first wanted to say thanks
to all of you for joining in

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.400
and much as we would have
loved to see you all in person

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.390
and have a lovely reception afterwards

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.330
at the moment we're gonna have to make do

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.943
with just the company and the talk.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.690
I wanted to remind you that
we are recording this event

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.790
so we request you all to mute your videos.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:47.610
And you can submit questions
to the speaker today

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.840
through the chat feature.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.410
And so we'll be reading
questions off the chat.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.410
And I wanna start off by
thanking our benefactors,

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.330
Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:05.330
who's support of both
the program materially

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.528
and of course the intellectual support

00:01:08.528 --> 00:01:11.180
is really deeply appreciated.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.380
They plan on joining us.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.913
I am not sure if they
have entered our room yet.

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:21.260
For today's talk, I'm utterly delighted

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.319
to introduce our distinguished speaker

00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:27.946
in this ongoing series,
mapping as knowing.

00:01:27.946 --> 00:01:29.750
And our speaker this evening

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.453
is Professor Frank Snowden.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:33.800
Professor Snowden is

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.140
the Andrew Downey Orrick
Professor Emeritus of History

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.700
and History of Medicine.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.990
And he'd received his PhD from Oxford.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:45.490
He is the author of many
critically acclaimed books,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.370
including Violence and Great Estates

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:52.750
in the South of Italy: Apulia 1900 - 1922

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.850
which was published in 1984.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.070
The Fascist Revolution
in Tuscany, 1919-1922,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.080
published in 1989.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.468
Naples in the Times of Cholera in 1995

00:02:04.468 --> 00:02:09.468
and The Conquest of Malaria
in Italy, from 1900-1962,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.800
a book that he published in 2006.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.050
You might wonder why I'm
reading out all these.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.430
This is just vicarious
pleasure from just imagining

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.740
the wonderful fieldwork experiences

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.900
that he must have had in
Apulia, Tuscany, Naples

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.590
and now that we're all stuck at home,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.990
I was really nostalgically
looking at this list

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.010
and wondering how what
it must really be like

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:34.933
to travel again.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:39.520
His book, The Conquest
of Malaria was awarded

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.880
the Gustav Ranis Prize

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.860
from the MacMillan center at Yale in 2007

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.942
as the best book on an international topic

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:48.337
by a member of the Yale faculty,

00:02:48.337 --> 00:02:51.460
an honor that you all
are probably aware of,

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.799
and the Helen and Howard Marraro Prize

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:55.998
by the American Historical Association

00:02:55.998 --> 00:02:59.990
as the best work on Italy in any period.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:04.710
He's the recipient of many
awards, accolades and honors.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.830
And I will just mention
one in the interest

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.980
of not holding you all up
from hearing him this evening.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.740
And that is the 2008 Welch Medal

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.860
from the American Association
for the History of Medicine.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.800
He is the author of a brand new book

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.900
which is extremely topical and timely

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.960
and has been very influential
since its publication

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.550
just earlier this year.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.820
And the book is titled
Epidemics and Society:

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.630
From the Black Death to the Present

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.160
and this is published by Yale Press.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.710
It's wonderfully written book

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:39.120
and I strongly encourage you to buy it.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.590
As you can see from the record

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.740
of his scholarship and his research

00:03:45.176 --> 00:03:48.400
he has deep insights on epidemics

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.133
and our ability or lack
of ability to manage them.

00:03:54.427 --> 00:03:55.260
(clears throat)

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.170
Today he will be talking to
us about maps and epidemics.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.520
In particular, lessons
for the current pandemic

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.010
for COVID-19.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.770
He also teaches undergraduate
and graduate courses

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.276
in Italian history, European
social and political history,

00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:14.900
and the history of medicine.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.190
Before I hand over to him

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.580
I just wanted to remind you

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.163
about a couple of upcoming Franke events.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:24.920
The next talk in our series on
the mapping as knowing series

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.430
is going to be by the
musicologist Victor Coelho

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.610
and that's going to be next term.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.110
You will all hear about it.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:36.110
We'll send you the poster
and details later on.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.887
I was very excited to announce that

00:04:38.887 --> 00:04:43.090
the Franke Program had announced
a contest called Eureka

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.700
which in light of the pandemic,

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.820
we have had to reimagine

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.500
and we have re-imagined
it as a new project

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.710
which we call Learning by Doing

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.830
which we are launching next week.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.800
It is a set of simple
