00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.091
We'll be collecting ideas
and we have a website.

00:05:06.091 --> 00:05:10.050
So please watch out for a website

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.800
www.learningbydoing.academy.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.110
I will share further
information in just a minute.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.110
Without further ado, I
would like to turn it over

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.970
to Professor Snowden to speak to us.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.780
We are eagerly waiting to learn from you

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.808
about maps and
epidemiology, in particular,

00:05:30.808 --> 00:05:35.210
lessons for this COVID-19

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.300
which has completely destabilized
our world at the moment.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.200
Professor Snowden.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:42.700
- Thank you so much.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:46.660
I thank all of you for
joining me this afternoon

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.170
and it's a great pleasure to be here.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.010
I wish also to thank the
Whitney Humanity Center

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.800
and the Franke Lecture
Series for inviting me

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.556
and especially I want to thank you

00:05:58.556 --> 00:06:01.600
for the very generous introduction.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.800
I was going to say a
word or two about myself

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.854
but I think that the
organizers know me much better

00:06:07.854 --> 00:06:09.470
than I know myself.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:14.460
So since that's no longer
necessary I'll just begin.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.020
And I'd like to say what my
plan is first this afternoon.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.020
What I'd like to do is to
consider the relationship

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.097
between historical map
making on the one hand

00:06:28.097 --> 00:06:32.190
and the emergence of
epidemiology on the other.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.381
My idea in particular is
to begin by considering

00:06:36.381 --> 00:06:40.580
one of the most influential
maps in the history of medicine,

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.910
the famous map of the
London-based physician, John Snow.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:48.980
He did in fact, draw two maps

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.200
and they've been called maps that changed

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.380
the history of the world.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.180
They helped to unlock the mystery

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.550
of Asiatic cholera as a disease.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.830
These were maps that also
related to the pandemic

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:08.440
that afflicted London in 1854.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:12.337
The first, and I can
perhaps show that now,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.100
is a map that was a detailed
map of a single neighborhood,

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.370
the neighborhood of Soho in London.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:24.980
The second map is a map, that's the one,

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.890
is the map of that I
won't actually be showing

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.120
but I will talk a little bit about it.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.680
It details the relationship
between the disease, cholera

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.730
and the provision of water to South London

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.480
by two different water companies.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.391
Having then explored
what Snow demonstrated

00:07:45.391 --> 00:07:47.740
by his cartography,

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:51.630
I wanna leap forward in time to COVID-19

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:56.110
thinking about what we can
learn about the epidemiology

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:00.957
of this pandemic by
considering a series of 239,

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:05.050
don't panic I'm not planning
to show you them all,

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.960
but 239 maps produced

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:11.700
by the United States
government in the 1930s.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.180
Maps that impose very important questions

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.140
about the nature of the Corona virus

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:20.920
and the ways in which it is laid bare.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.630
The way that social injustice itself

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:27.886
is a very important pathway
through which this disease

00:08:27.886 --> 00:08:31.220
is making its way through our country.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.066
Now, let's remember when
we talk about John Snow,

00:08:35.066 --> 00:08:38.800
what the understanding of Asiatic cholera

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.240
was at the time when he
was carrying out his work.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:45.670
At that time, the international
medical profession

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:50.220
had two different views
regarding what cholera might be.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:54.860
The dominant view was
one that was the view

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.920
that this was not a contagious disease,

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:02.520
it was an anti contagionist view.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.084
It argued that cholera was not transmitted

00:09:06.084 --> 00:09:09.655
from person to person in any sort of way

00:09:09.655 --> 00:09:14.655
but in some undefined way
it was produced by a poison

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.930
in the air called the miasma.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.620
The miasma originated

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.830
from decaying organic matter and filth.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.270
And the miasma arose as a deadly effluvium

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:31.610
that susceptible people
inhaled and fell ill.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:36.610
In England it was very much
common by medical authorities

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.300
to refer to malaria, that is bad air,

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.990
or they also talked about
the filth theory of disease.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.350
That is to say whole
categories of disease,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:54.350
including cholera, were
thought to be non-contagious.

00:09:54.588 --> 00:09:59.588
Contagionism, the idea
of an infectious disease,

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.712
was a minority view but the one

00:10:01.712 --> 00:10:05.840
that John Snow himself espoused.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.270
He believed that cholera was not

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.560
a miasmatic disease at all

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.130
but was transmitted person to person

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:16.740
via the medium of contaminated water.

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.920
In 1849 he had already written a book

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.700
on the mode of communication of cholera

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:25.000
before his famous maps.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:29.671
And there he outlined his
objections to miasmatism

00:10:29.671 --> 00:10:33.200
and proposed instead what he saw

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.320
as the more plausible
interpretation of contagion.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.800
But he lacked convincing evidence

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.280
and couldn't suggest
the mechanisms above all

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:46.960
that were involved in
his idea of transmission.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.630
So the major outbreak of 1854

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:52.890
provided him with the occasion

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.510
to do very careful detective work

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:57.710
and to produce two maps

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:00.650
of the first of which we might return to.

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:05.650
This map is the neighborhood
of Soho in 1854.

00:11:07.976 --> 00:11:10.880
Soho experienced at that time

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:14.870
an intense local outbreak
in the neighborhood

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:19.460
that cholera was what he called

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:23.880
the worst disease in the
history of the kingdom.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.410
At the time, Soho had no sewer system.

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.820
It had no secure supply of Water

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:34.810
and most houses had re coursed to cesspits

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:38.080
and residents collected water for drinking

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.480
and for their ablutions and
buckets from a pump well

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.650
and that pump well was where
the red arrow in the map is,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:51.100
in Broad Street located at
the center of the district.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.950
Now Snow's suspicions focused on the pump.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:59.410
And to test his idea he carried
out careful detective work

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.860
mapping cases of cholera,
these are spot maps,

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:07.363
and each of the black areas that you see

00:12:07.363 --> 00:12:12.363
represents cases of cholera
in the neighborhood of Soho

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:16.980
and a number of features
clearly stood out.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.261
The first was that the nearer
people were to the pump,

00:12:20.261 --> 00:12:24.253
the more likely a house was
to have cases of cholera.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.020
A second, was not people
who depended on the pump

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:33.870
for their drinking water
fell ill first of all.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.040
Then there were some puzzling factors

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.124
that took quite a bit of detective work.

00:12:38.124 --> 00:12:43.124
There were 10 puzzling cases
of people who lived nearby

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:47.770
and fell ill of cholera but
never took water from the pump.

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:51.790
But it turned out that they ate meals

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:53.740
at a restaurant nearby

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.730
and the restaurant served not only food,

00:12:56.730 --> 00:13:00.670
but it's served it's diners water fresh

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.010
from the Broad Street pump.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:04.670
Then there was another puzzle

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.960
was that there was a jail nearby

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.910
and somehow none of the 200 prisoners

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:12.043
had fallen ill with cholera again.

00:13:12.043 --> 00:13:14.810
It turned out that in fact,

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:19.340
the prison brought in water
from a distance source

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:23.410
and so the prisoners
didn't drink water at all.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:27.373
And then by an interview with
a local minister in Soho,

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:32.890
what John Snow learned
was a crucial further fact

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:35.800
that is the minister pointed into

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:40.350
what modern epidemiologists
might call patient zero.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.277
It turned out that a local woman

00:13:42.277 --> 00:13:44.560
who'd been away from the area

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.393
had contracted cholera but then recovered.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.970
She returned to Soho with her toddler

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.560
who also fell violently ill.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.100
She disposed of the infant's diapers

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.130
in a cesspit with porous walls

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:03.023
located just a few feet
from the well itself.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.520
So Snow examined the well water

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.853
and discovered white flicks floating in it

00:14:09.853 --> 00:14:12.763
that he thought were
the pathogenic material.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:17.820
We know from modern studies

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:21.290
and our knowledge of the
symptomatology of cholera

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.830
that it produces the
famous rice waters stools

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.870
which are in fact, the blood plasma

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:32.870
being drawn out of the
the intestinal system

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.610
and along with that some of the lining

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:38.410
of the intestinal lumen

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:43.133
which actually is the white
flakes that snow discovered.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:47.640
Having rapidly collected
and mapped his findings,

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:50.320
Snow was able to overcome

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.720
the resistance of local authorities

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:54.910
to try a further experiment

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:59.480
which was to deactivate the
suspect well in Broad Street

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.920
by removing the pump handle

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:04.737
and when they finally concurred,

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:07.030
they removed the handle

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.063
and the disease immediately ceased.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:16.700
Retrospectively after this epidemic,

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.010
Snow continued his research.

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:22.920
This time doing detective
work in South London.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:27.170
Not all of London relied
on hand pumps and wells.

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:31.700
Some districts were served
by private water companies.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:33.850
And Snow drew up a map.

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:35.500
I'll leave you to imagine it.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.620
You can just imagine a
map of the city of London

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:41.930
with the river coursing through

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:46.650
and then various areas on
the South side of the river

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:50.360
with different colors
for different districts

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.839
that were supplied by
different water companies.

00:15:53.839 --> 00:15:56.770
It turns out there were two in particular,

00:15:56.770 --> 00:16:00.010
the Lambeth Water
Company and the Southwark

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:02.380
and Vauxhall Water Company.

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:05.810
And whether you received
water from one or the other

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:09.163
made a tremendous difference
in what happened to you.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:14.720
Let's start with the Southwark
and Vauxhall Water Company.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:19.720
It serves 40,000 houses
and it yielded 1,213 cases

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:26.660
of cholera or 315 for
every 10,000 houses served.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.360
The Southwork and Vauxhall Water Company

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.030
drew its water from the river towns

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:38.410
long after it had begun its
pathway through the city

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:41.250
and after the residents of London

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:45.710
had already disposed of
their waste in its waters.

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:48.630
The Lambeth company by contrast

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:53.630
drew its water upstream
before it had entered the city

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:59.200
and it produced just 98 cases
of cholera for 27,000 houses.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:05.660
That is a rate of 37
for every 10,000 houses

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:09.620
versus 315 for the other water company.

00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:14.620
So the impact of Snow's map
was very, very important.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:18.000
The two maps marked the foundation

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.980
of the discipline of epidemiology

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:24.690
and showed the utility of spot maps.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:27.400
They also had a very practical impact

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:29.150
on the sanitary movement

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:32.660
that was just then getting
underway in Britain.

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:36.160
It gave a boost to contagionism as well

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.330
although the medical
profession remained skeptical

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.860
and said this was a matter of correlation

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:44.780
rather than causation

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:47.141
and they complained that Snow had yet

00:17:47.141 --> 00:17:52.141
to discover the actual pathogen
itself to prove his case

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.720
and that in fact, we now
know required the development

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:03.720
in the next generation of
microscopy as a field of research.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:08.080
But Snow's hypothesis
was ultimately confirmed

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.270
by the work of Pasteur and
Robert Koch marking the triumph

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:16.290
of the germ theory of
disease by the 1880s.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:20.170
And for our purposes a
feature that's very important

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.900
is that it demonstrated in practice

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:26.600
the power of map making
from medical science.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.760
The application in fact,

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.500
of some of the tools of the humanities

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:35.190
in this case illustration
and storytelling.

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:37.650
Because that's what the maps were doing.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.040
They were telling a narrative

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:43.650
to elaborating the
scientific understanding

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.560
of this deadly disease,

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:48.743
the most feared disease
of the 19th century.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:51.900
I'm going to fast forward

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:55.770
and return again to the 21st century

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:58.300
and begin with my third map

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:00.600
which is that I want to talk about

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:03.283
which would be the next
slide if I could have that.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:08.280
This is a map.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:09.790
This is the one.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:14.790
It's one of the 239 maps
that I warned you about.

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:17.440
Relax because this is the only one

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:19.340
I'm actually going to show you.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:23.760
It had two lives and
had two very different

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:26.773
but powerful impacts on our country.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:32.030
The first impact was during the 1930s

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:37.030
when these maps served the
purpose of an enormous plan

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:41.810
of social engineering
carried out by the New Deal

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:46.100
and the United States
government in the 1930s

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:50.603
for purposes that are now
recognized as profoundly racist.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:55.500
More recently, these
maps had been resurrected

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:59.620
and it provided an
understanding of vital aspects

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:03.826
of the current coronavirus
pandemic in our country.

00:20:03.826 --> 00:20:08.230
They have followed investigative methods,

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:13.230
various commentators, various
methods highly reminiscent

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:18.250
of Snow's methodology in the 19th century.

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:21.042
Now this particular map is a map

00:20:21.042 --> 00:20:26.042
of the 1930s of Philadelphia,

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:31.320
And you'll note the different
colored areas on it.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:37.110
The maps demonstrate that
considering these maps

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:43.930
is worthy of a Franke series lecture

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:46.400
because they bring together disciplines

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:51.060
that normally remain in
separate disciplinary silos.

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:54.780
I'm thinking of environmental
science, history,

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:57.960
epidemiology, and cartography.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:02.840
And the maps also raise
urgent and practical questions

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:07.330
of politics, ethics and public health.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:12.330
What lessons do we learn from
COVID-19 about its origins

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:15.770
and capacity to spread in our midst?

00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:18.690
How do we learn enough both to emerge

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:21.420
on the other side of this pandemic

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:25.470
and to prepare ourselves for
future epidemic challenges

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:28.530
by removing the crucial vulnerabilities

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:33.320
that caught us so unprepared
at the start of 2020?

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:36.860
And then there's a
great question of ethics

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:39.393
and how we deal with what we learn.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:43.770
A great interest in pandemics, I believe,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.840
is that they are among other things,

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:50.188
great moral dramas, taking
place before our eyes

00:21:50.188 --> 00:21:53.770
and laying bare the nature
of our relationships

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:55.550
to one another.

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:58.170
Pandemics are not random.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:02.340
They reflect the nature of
the society we have built

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:04.179
and they travel on pathways

00:22:04.179 --> 00:22:08.490
that we ourselves have
constructed for them

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:12.050
as they make their way through our midst.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:17.020
If those ideas are correct
then what do we see

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:20.960
in this particular reflection of ourselves

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.360
that we'll see in this map?

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:26.600
And what ethical and political imperatives

00:22:26.600 --> 00:22:29.323
do we feel to change the picture?

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.930
Now that we're still plunged
in the midst of our tragedy,

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.360
this may indeed be the best time

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:38.660
to reflect on how we got here

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:43.410
and what needs to change to
make our society COVID proof

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:45.560
for the next generation.

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:49.470
But first of all let's turn to the maps.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.930
They're now fairly well-known.

00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:54.140
This is one of the collection.

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.660
The collection was discovered
in the national archives

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:00.560
and they have now all been digitized

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:04.340
and are presently housed at
the University of Richmond

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:06.950
and are available online.

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:10.490
Their purposes and impact
have also been explored

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:13.800
most notably recently by Richard Rothstein

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:16.900
in his wonderful book, The Color of Law

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:19.953
and also by Ta-Nehisi Coates.

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:26.630
These maps were the means
to implement, as I said,

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:31.540
a vast program of social
engineering by the New Deal.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.910
In other words, Franklin Roosevelt

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:37.610
and his administration to
combat the great depression

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:40.190
and boost housing construction

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:45.190
and to improve the living
standards of the poor

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:49.600
and working classes by a
redistribution of wealth.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:52.920
That set up three federal agencies

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:57.350
that were interrelated and
all had to do with housing.

00:23:57.350 --> 00:24:01.415
The Public Works
Administration set up in 1933,

00:24:01.415 --> 00:24:05.430
the Homeowners Loan
Corporation of the same year

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:10.430
and the FHA, the Federal
Housing Administration in 1934.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:16.050
These carried out an enormous
redistribution of wealth

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.530
to promote construction and home ownership

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:21.830
that was at this time considered

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:24.610
the embodiment of the American dream

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:28.600
and a bulwark against
possible left-wing subversion

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:32.610
as homeowners, it was argued,
would never risk communism

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.113
and social subversion.

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:38.640
But at the same time, the
Roosevelt administration

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:42.370
fully embraced American racial hierarchies

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:44.950
and intended to institutionalize

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:47.910
and reinforce segregation.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:51.167
Billions of federal dollars were deployed

00:24:51.167 --> 00:24:55.030
to rescue distressed white homeowners

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.630
or aspiring white homeowners

00:24:57.630 --> 00:25:02.630
by means of cheap government
backed mortgages and insurance.

00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:06.750
To that end the cheap
mortgages under the program

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:10.730
were not to be available
to African-Americans

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:14.693
and the maps with a means
to implement that vision.

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:20.300
The Homeowners Loan
Corporation commissioned maps,

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:23.100
therefore, of every major city

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:27.100
and town in the country, 239 in all

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:29.100
and it's just important to stress

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.956
that these were carried
out not just in the South

00:25:31.956 --> 00:25:33.910
but in the North as well.

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:35.840
And this is a good example.

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:38.420
It's a map of Philadelphia.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:43.033
In these maps, each
neighborhood of each city

00:25:43.033 --> 00:25:48.033
was graded and shaded and
then coded for the purpose

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:53.080
of qualifying applicants
for mortgages and insurance.

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:57.020
And the coding provided
direction for the entire mortgage

00:25:57.020 --> 00:26:00.960
and insurance industry,
banks, savings institutions,

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:04.930
savings and loan associations,
insurance companies.

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:08.360
Green earned you a grade of A.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.430
This marked the best
neighborhoods where only whites

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:15.050
were intended to live
in single-family houses

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:17.920
and where subsidized and insured mortgages

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.850
were to be made available.

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.513
We should realize that these maps

00:26:22.513 --> 00:26:27.513
were not simply descriptions
of what existed.

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:32.020
They were prescriptions of
what was intended to be.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:37.020
So the next below the
green was blue graded B.

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:40.674
These were also to be Lily white,

00:26:40.674 --> 00:26:44.300
but they were less desirable areas.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:49.030
Lower down the yellow areas on
the map earned a grade of C,

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:51.970
there was somewhat
hazardous for investors.

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:56.870
Areas that perhaps had what
documents accompanying the maps

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.587
called, and I'm quoting here,

00:26:59.587 --> 00:27:03.067
"unwelcome infiltrations of Negroes

00:27:03.067 --> 00:27:05.370
"and foreign born people."

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:07.970
And the mixing, and I'm quoting again,

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:11.620
of imharmonius ethnic groupings.

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:15.342
And then at the bottom
there's red, the grade of D.

00:27:15.342 --> 00:27:18.770
And this is the area
where loans, investments,

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:23.453
and insurance were all to
be rigidly discouraged.

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:28.120
The red zones gave rise
to the term redlining

00:27:28.120 --> 00:27:31.380
but as you can see the
neighborhood divisions

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:33.910
are actually outlined in block

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:37.420
and the term therefore
is slightly appropriate.

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.510
The areas are distinguished by shading

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:42.760
in the relevant colors.

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:46.950
So the maps were proscriptive.

00:27:46.950 --> 00:27:51.610
The color codes were intended
to regularize segregation

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:54.470
and housing on a permanent basis

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:57.970
and in fact, they were
eminently successful in that

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:01.330
and that's precisely what they achieved.

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:04.590
And the racial and
nativist vision involved

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:06.390
was in no way hidden.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:11.350
Each map was accompanied by
a one page appraisal document

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:16.050
that specified that Negroes
in foreign born undesirables

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:19.360
were not eligible for
loans and home ownership

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:24.000
and that investment in the area
was officially discouraged.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:28.160
Furthermore, the homes
purchased were to provide

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:29.830
in their property deed,

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.630
what were termed restrictive covenants.

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:35.670
That is clauses that in perpetuity

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:40.240
excluded the sale of the
property to African-Americans.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:44.490
These clauses had already
been tested in 1926

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:45.956
before the Supreme Court

00:28:45.956 --> 00:28:50.690
which pronounced such covenants
legal and constitutional.

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:53.530
So segregation was meant to be permanent

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.330
throughout the land.

00:28:55.330 --> 00:28:59.570
And informally on a local
basis in both North and South,

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:03.610
attempts by African-Americans
other professional classes

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:05.410
to break the color wall

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.860
and to move into areas

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:12.650
that were not shaded in the proper color

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:16.550
were often met with
violence, riot, and fire

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:18.390
as we know, for example,

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:21.254
from the Cicero neighborhood of Chicago.

00:29:21.254 --> 00:29:23.800
And this shows us that racism

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:26.670
isn't simply a matter of attitudes.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.216
The whites of Cicero,

00:29:28.216 --> 00:29:31.500
when they attacked African-Americans,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.571
knew full well that the arrival

00:29:33.571 --> 00:29:36.506
of an African-American
family in their midst

00:29:36.506 --> 00:29:39.477
meant recoding of the area

00:29:39.477 --> 00:29:44.140
and therefore a precipitous
fall in property values.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:48.410
Racism, therefore was illegal
in financial structure

00:29:48.410 --> 00:29:49.873
and not just an attitude.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:52.900
But now you'll be wondering

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:56.223
how were the maps relevant to COVID-19.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.840
To understand that we need to realize

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:04.240
that the racial segregation
engineered in the 1930s

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:06.710
is a structural racist vision

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:10.730
that persists to this very
day during the pandemic.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:15.080
The Federal Reserve Bank
of Chicago, for example,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:17.502
noted in a study in 1910

00:30:17.502 --> 00:30:20.086
that the pattern of racial disparity

00:30:20.086 --> 00:30:24.000
established by red lining was persisting

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.900
80 years after it had been first proposed

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:31.550
in terms of segregation,
home ownership ratios,

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:34.390
house values, and credit ratings.

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:36.832
And in fact, one could go further

00:30:36.832 --> 00:30:39.650
than the Federal Reserve of Chicago

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:43.040
by saying that actually
the position had worsened

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:45.350
rather than improving.

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.900
That is to say after World War II

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.887
we see the great
suburbanization of America

00:30:50.887 --> 00:30:53.530
that was funded by the GI bill

00:30:53.530 --> 00:30:55.990
and the veterans administration.

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:59.400
And this again redistributed
billions of dollars

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:02.470
to white veterans to purchase new homes

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:05.680
but again not to African-Americans

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:09.160
however bravely they
had fought in the war.

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:13.500
The legal and lending
practices of the 1930s

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:16.450
originally confined to the cities

00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:19.690
were now exported also to suburbia

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:22.950
complete with the restrictive covenants.

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:25.950
And we get closer to
public health and disease

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:29.330
by noting that the New
Deal Housing Program

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:33.840
and the Veteran Administration
Program after world war II

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:37.710
were powerful engines
of economic disparity

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.150
and the creation of poverty.

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:43.600
It's not by coincidence
that the wealth possessed

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:47.240
by the average
African-American family today

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:50.540
in the United States is 5%

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:53.373
of that owned by the average white family.

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:59.550
What that means is that there
has been a publicly subsidized

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:06.070
and persistent equity in home ownership

00:32:06.070 --> 00:32:10.890
from the 1930s onward
that has been directed

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:15.660
towards particular racial
groups and denied others.

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:19.070
This was a decisive creator of wealth

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:22.610
and African-Americans were systematically

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:26.150
and purposefully deprived of access to it

00:32:26.150 --> 00:32:30.860
as well as those wealth creating
mechanisms of the tax laws

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:34.470
that provided deductions
for mortgage payments

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:38.400
and the inheritance laws that
enabled accumulated wealth

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:42.800
to be passed with little risk
from generation to generation.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:44.690
Let me give you an example.

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:49.690
Let me turn to the very ideal
type of the new suburbia

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:53.590
that emerged after World War II,

00:32:53.590 --> 00:32:56.520
their very first great suburban community

00:32:56.520 --> 00:33:01.030
that is Levittown and
Nassau County, New York.

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:04.110
There, 17,000 homes were built.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:07.400
I should note too, that they
were intentionally built

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:09.690
by non-unionized labor

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:14.560
and during the construction
the builders were picketed.

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:19.397
They were offered in
1957 for sale at $7,990.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:23.340
We can say were offered

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:26.530
because these houses were prefabricated.

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:29.470
There were assembled in 26 pieces

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:34.170
and each one was identical
a cape or a ranch

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:37.320
to all of those on the surrounding block.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:41.380
So they were for sale all
at once for $7,990 each.

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:47.590
Made available, however,
to veterans at $400.

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:54.610
In 2018 the exact same prefabricated homes

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:58.700
were worth an average of $400,000.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:03.190
So you can see the enormous
accumulation in equity

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:08.190
between 1957 and 2020 and
realize the way in which

00:34:10.197 --> 00:34:14.480
the wealth of white families
was publicly subsidized

00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:17.130
and denied to African-Americans.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:20.219
As we know in the areas shaded in red,

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:23.720
African-Americans continued to languish

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:27.803
in multi-family dilapidated
inner city neighborhoods.

00:34:29.410 --> 00:34:33.120
There were some countervailing influences.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:35.560
The Supreme court in 1948

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:37.690
declared the restrictive covenants

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:40.360
a violation of the 14th amendment

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:45.350
and the Fair Housing Act of
1968 made red lining illegal

00:34:45.350 --> 00:34:47.070
in the mortgage industry.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.246
But in practice the
old structures remained

00:34:50.246 --> 00:34:52.610
and were not dismantled.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.030
So the former redlined neighborhoods

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:58.700
are today's inner city ghettos.

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:03.200
The very ones that were
ravaged by the coronavirus.

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:06.060
We could say then, that the red shadings

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:09.590
on the maps of the 1930s still define

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:13.180
the islands of vulnerability
to infectious disease

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.340
and death in 2020.

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:19.360
I would like to show
the last show and tell

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:20.610
that I'm going to present

00:35:21.601 --> 00:35:24.790
which follows the map that I showed you.

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:27.850
You won't be able to see
it very well I'm afraid.

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:31.306
The point is that this is one
of the appraisal documents

00:35:31.306 --> 00:35:34.170
that accompanied the maps.

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:37.240
Each Mount had this document along with it

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:41.470
explaining what the neighborhood
was meant to look like

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:43.540
and we'll see, for example,

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:46.240
this will be familiar to you all.

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:50.540
It's a de-rated neighborhood
in New Haven, Connecticut,

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:55.540
and it goes along Gulf,
Whaley and Dixwell Avenues

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.210
as far as Sherman Avenues

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:01.801
and in the 1930s it tells us

00:36:01.801 --> 00:36:06.020
that the inhabitants there were domestics

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:09.040
and that they were predominantly Negro.

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:11.860
There was even a line to
tell if you were Negro

00:36:11.860 --> 00:36:13.220
or foreign born.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:16.130
That is the nativism and the racism.

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:21.130
It's 70% Negroes and in
growing it says infiltration.

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:25.030
Negroes are infiltrating the area.

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:28.910
And it tells us that these are houses

00:36:28.910 --> 00:36:32.450
that are predominantly multifamily

00:36:32.450 --> 00:36:35.620
and not a single owner occupied.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:38.970
And at the bottom for clarifying remarks

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:42.290
it says, this is an
older section of the city

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:45.641
which has now given
over largely to Negroes

00:36:45.641 --> 00:36:48.340
employed as domestics.

00:36:48.340 --> 00:36:53.340
Dwelling from small
singles to multifamily.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:37:00.180
This is quite congested as an area

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:04.440
and it gives the
appearance of a slum area.

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:06.878
There's an absence of market forces

00:37:06.878 --> 00:37:11.440
that has resulted in some demolitions

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:15.100
and the section is subject to vandalism

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:18.350
and we'll see that its
prediction for the future

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:21.230
is that it's going to decline further

00:37:21.230 --> 00:37:24.710
and it says, is there any
availability of mortgage?

00:37:24.710 --> 00:37:28.410
And this was a self
fulfilling prophecy of none.

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:31.200
So that's just to show you

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:33.900
that this was a very intentional plan

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:38.380
and that the racial narrative
was completely built

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:40.983
into the structure of the plan.

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:45.500
The reasons for the disparities today

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:48.621
and vulnerability to COVID-19

00:37:48.621 --> 00:37:51.690
are not all of course attributable

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:54.790
to the legacy of structural racism.

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:57.777
But that goes a long way to explaining

00:37:57.777 --> 00:38:00.970
what's happening today in our midst.

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:03.510
Some of the sources of vulnerability

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:05.800
are not immediately related.

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:08.340
School segregation, for example,

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:10.900
means that houses are underfunded

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:14.060
and schools provide
people with the knowledge

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:16.740
that they need to protect themselves.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:21.240
Access to medical care
has to do with insurance

00:38:22.819 --> 00:38:27.425
or underinsurance and also
with the legacy of distrust

00:38:27.425 --> 00:38:30.510
between the African-American community

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:34.490
and the medical profession
which emerged so starkly

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:37.420
as a result of the Tuskegee experiment.

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:41.530
We could talk also about
unemployment and wages,

00:38:41.530 --> 00:38:44.290
about types of work, about diet

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:49.290
and the widespread presence
of obesity and diabetes

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:53.960
that are predisposing factors for COVID-19

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:55.680
and one could extend the list.

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:58.490
But what I would like to do is to note

00:38:58.490 --> 00:39:03.490
how many factors are related
to what was done in the 1930s

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:07.500
and confirmed after World War II.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:10.250
The first point is that the housing stock

00:39:10.250 --> 00:39:12.610
in the inner cities, as we all know,

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:16.600
has been poorly maintained
and often unhygienic.

00:39:16.600 --> 00:39:19.110
That there's multiple family occupancy,

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:22.890
multiple generation occupancy as well

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:27.110
and we know that COVID-19 makes its way

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:30.560
preferentially among elderly bodies

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:35.560
and also that it's a viral
disease spread by droplets

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:39.320
and aerosols through the air

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:43.390
and therefore it thrives
on conditions of density

00:39:43.390 --> 00:39:46.760
and these are densely packed, multifamily,

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:49.590
multi-generational houses.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:53.910
We also know that African-American homes,

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:58.770
these neighborhoods had
a shortage of amenities

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:02.760
and some actually have
been often disconnected

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:03.593
such as water in our country.

00:40:03.593 --> 00:40:08.140
There's a disproportionate
number of families

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:10.620
without household water supplies

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:14.080
and therefore unable to
practice hand washing,

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:19.080
one of the major bits of
advice for coping with COVID-19

00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:25.080
and also because of the
density of incapable

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:27.870
of practicing social distancing.

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:32.320
Another major factor is environmental.

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:34.180
And this is directly related

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:40.230
and built upon the social
engineering of the New Deal.

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:45.080
That is green and red shaded neighborhoods

00:40:45.080 --> 00:40:50.080
actually constitute different
ecological environments.

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:56.180
One feature that's most
important is air pollution.

00:40:57.910 --> 00:41:02.860
By air pollution I mean, on
the one hand greenhouse gases,

00:41:02.860 --> 00:41:07.860
that is ground level ozone or
the various oxides of nitrogen

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:14.260
emitted by cars, heating, industry,

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:20.217
refineries, power plants,
decaying tyres, landfills

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:25.300
and recently here at Yale,
there's been a discovery

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:29.180
of the vital importance of urban land use.

00:41:29.180 --> 00:41:33.340
That is to say in a grade A neighborhood

00:41:33.340 --> 00:41:38.340
there were not what you
find in a green neighborhood

00:41:38.427 --> 00:41:41.190
in a grade D red neighborhood.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:45.230
You would not find the amenities
of a grade A neighborhood

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:49.340
in terms of large yards, parks, trees.

00:41:49.340 --> 00:41:51.730
Instead in the inner cities,

00:41:51.730 --> 00:41:56.730
approximately 50% of the
land is covered in asphalt

00:41:56.850 --> 00:41:59.710
for roads, roofs, and driveways

00:41:59.710 --> 00:42:04.110
and in the heat they emit enormous amounts

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:07.110
of greenhouse gases

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:10.570
and global warming is
making the position worse.

00:42:10.570 --> 00:42:15.090
It's generating what
are called heat islands

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:17.040
in the center of the cities

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:21.220
that are five to 20
degrees Fahrenheit warmer

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:24.810
than the A rated areas were.

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:28.723
And an ultra fine particulate matter

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:34.793
generated by industry,
coal burning, wildfires,

00:42:34.793 --> 00:42:38.850
construction, and demolition,
crumbling plaster,

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:39.860
and all the rest.

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:43.250
And it's interesting to
note the citing of industry

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:47.500
was in part dependent on the zoning,

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:50.530
the red lining of the 1930s.

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:53.643
That is to say that the most noxious

00:42:53.643 --> 00:42:58.420
fuel producing industries and services

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:03.420
were called fence line
industries directly adjacent

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:08.270
to a de-rated neighborhood.

00:43:08.270 --> 00:43:10.600
That is to say there were areas

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.750
where the land was cheaper for placement

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:16.920
where there were probably
highways near bond.

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:19.180
There were low transaction costs.

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:22.460
It was easy and cheap
to get zoning permission

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:25.940
with little opposition
from impoverished residents

00:43:25.940 --> 00:43:27.930
with little political power.

00:43:27.930 --> 00:43:31.195
South Philadelphia, on the
map I showed you before,

00:43:31.195 --> 00:43:36.195
South Philadelphia is a
vast sea of red on the map

00:43:36.800 --> 00:43:40.836
and the point is that it
was a fence line community

00:43:40.836 --> 00:43:43.740
inhabited by African-Americans.

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:47.892
It's located next to
the largest oil refinery

00:43:47.892 --> 00:43:51.631
until last year in the
East of the United States.

00:43:51.631 --> 00:43:55.440
It has incinerators,
sewage treatment plants,

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:57.860
power plants, and chemical companies.

00:43:57.860 --> 00:44:02.860
None of what you see in the
blue and white shaded areas

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:05.950
to the top of the map.

00:44:05.950 --> 00:44:10.730
The NAACP set up a clean air task force

00:44:10.730 --> 00:44:12.987
in which had noted that African-Americans

00:44:12.987 --> 00:44:17.987
are 75% more likely to live
in a fence line community

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.699
than a white American

00:44:20.699 --> 00:44:24.111
and that they are on average exposed

00:44:24.111 --> 00:44:28.170
to more than 50% more air pollution

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:31.160
on a constant basis.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:36.160
Now air pollution as a leading
cause of death worldwide.

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:41.290
7 million people around the
globe die prematurely every year

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:44.370
from the impact of air pollution

00:44:44.370 --> 00:44:47.750
according to the World
Health Organization.

00:44:47.750 --> 00:44:50.830
And that is more people than perish

00:44:50.830 --> 00:44:55.830
from malaria, TB, HIV/AIDS,
and warfare all combined.

00:44:56.400 --> 00:45:01.400
These pollution generates
stroke, asthma, heart disease,

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:08.070
respiratory infections,
obstructive lung diseases, cancers.

00:45:08.670 --> 00:45:12.400
But air pollution is also a specific cause

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:15.680
of vulnerability to COVID-19.

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:19.887
A source of the very comorbidities
that lead to severe death

00:45:19.887 --> 00:45:22.573
and to severe disease.

00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:27.360
Fine particulate matter destroys the cilia

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:30.690
of the upper respiratory pathways

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:35.630
and it causes inflammation
in the inner respiratory trap

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:38.840
of bronchioles and alveoli.

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:42.460
And recent research has done what Snow did

00:45:42.460 --> 00:45:47.320
which was carried out at
Harvard, in the Netherlands

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:50.730
and in the Nepal Valley in Italy,

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:53.863
they have followed Snow's mythology

00:45:53.863 --> 00:45:58.530
of superimposing maps of COVID mortality

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:03.530
and morbidity over the
counties in the United States

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:06.705
and the administrative districts in Europe

00:46:06.705 --> 00:46:10.200
that had the worst air pollution.

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:13.340
Unfortunately these maps aren't available

00:46:13.340 --> 00:46:15.230
for public use at the moment

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:17.850
and so we'll just have
to imagine something

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:20.360
like what Snow did.

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:23.720
It turns out that there's
a direct correlation

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:26.550
just as in the case of
the Broad Street pump

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:30.410
and the South water
companies in South London

00:46:30.410 --> 00:46:35.410
between these vulnerabilities
and to disease.

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:42.620
Particularly the infectious
disease cholera in Snow's case,

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:45.570
COVID-19 in ours.

00:46:45.570 --> 00:46:49.229
And then physiological studies

00:46:49.229 --> 00:46:54.010
are increasingly demonstrating
direct mechanisms

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:56.525
especially that of inflammation

00:46:56.525 --> 00:47:01.525
that are directly related to
vulnerability to COVID-19.

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:07.458
So now that I'm running
rapidly out of time,

00:47:07.458 --> 00:47:10.283
I think it would be wise to note,

00:47:11.410 --> 00:47:14.750
with a hopeful note towards the end,

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:19.750
that we are waiting now for a
vaccine that will soon arrive

00:47:21.210 --> 00:47:24.310
but I do want to make a caveat.

00:47:24.310 --> 00:47:28.120
At the same time it's
important for us to remember

00:47:28.120 --> 00:47:33.120
that both for COVID-19 and even
more perhaps for the future,

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:37.902
we need to address social
determinants of disease

00:47:37.902 --> 00:47:41.560
that are critical to our preparedness.

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:44.363
This should happen in
part because it's right,

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:49.110
but also because the
pandemic has demonstrated

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:53.470
that it is also an act of
enlightened self-interest.

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:57.130
All of us in other words,
are in this together.

00:47:57.130 --> 00:48:01.300
And if some communities are
at especially high risk,

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:04.483
everyone else will also be in danger.

00:48:05.341 --> 00:48:10.341
Map making reminds us what
Paul Farmer has eloquently said

00:48:12.172 --> 00:48:16.260
that medicine and public
health need to focus

00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:19.490
not only on molecular level phenomenon

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:25.160
but need to give due
attention to social factors

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:27.900
and make structural interventions

00:48:27.900 --> 00:48:31.800
that doctors, nurses,
and public health workers

00:48:31.800 --> 00:48:35.901
are in fact, the natural
advocates of the poor,

00:48:35.901 --> 00:48:40.120
the disadvantaged and
the racially oppressed.

00:48:40.120 --> 00:48:44.110
So I think that this is
a practical public health

00:48:44.110 --> 00:48:48.800
and a moral imperative that faces us

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:51.410
and I hope that as a country

00:48:51.410 --> 00:48:55.420
even after the vaccine has done its work

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:58.720
will remember what got
us into the situation

00:48:58.720 --> 00:48:59.900
in the first place

00:48:59.900 --> 00:49:03.180
and remember that this is almost certainly

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:07.667
not the last challenge from viral disease

00:49:08.720 --> 00:49:10.130
that we'll be facing

00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:13.300
and that we need to be
prepared in the future

00:49:13.300 --> 00:49:18.300
and not allow unnecessary
suffering and disaster

00:49:19.342 --> 00:49:24.342
that has ravaged our
communities over this past year.

00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:29.080
Now if people have objections or questions

00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:34.100
that would be wonderful.

00:49:34.100 --> 00:49:37.220
- Thank you very much for
that really thoughtful

00:49:37.220 --> 00:49:39.880
and illuminating talk.

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:41.786
I think the chat is open.

00:49:41.786 --> 00:49:46.786
Please go ahead and
type in your questions.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:50.600
And I guess while we wait for that

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:55.600
I might just go ahead and
ask you a question myself.

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:04.430
I think that it is very clear
historically as you showed

00:50:04.430 --> 00:50:07.300
that the patterns of human settlement

00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:12.300
and socioeconomic inequalities
that undergird that

00:50:12.590 --> 00:50:17.590
really do shape the way in
which diseases propagate.

00:50:18.517 --> 00:50:23.460
If you could think of one
thing that we could do

00:50:23.460 --> 00:50:26.520
as a society at the policy level,

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:30.290
what would it be after COVID-19?

00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:34.194
Obviously inequities in society

00:50:34.194 --> 00:50:37.040
and economic inequalities
have really become

00:50:37.040 --> 00:50:39.763
much stocker in the
last couple of decades.

00:50:41.330 --> 00:50:46.280
Do you think that it is time
now to seriously address that

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:50.066
because it is going to be
it would offer the backdrop

00:50:50.066 --> 00:50:55.066
for any future pandemics or
any kinds of other catastrophes

00:50:56.250 --> 00:50:59.140
that are not individual tragedies

00:50:59.140 --> 00:51:04.140
but if climate change
induced catastrophes as well,

00:51:04.210 --> 00:51:08.515
I was curious if you had
advice for policymakers

00:51:08.515 --> 00:51:12.500
as to what would be a key intervention now

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:14.604
in light of what we've learned so far.

00:51:14.604 --> 00:51:16.350
- Right.

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:21.350
I think that, since you've
limited me to one intervention--

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.210
- No, no, actually I take it back.

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:25.077
- One is fine.

00:51:25.077 --> 00:51:27.170
I will take you at your word

00:51:27.170 --> 00:51:29.930
and say that I would think of one

00:51:29.930 --> 00:51:34.710
that addresses directly the issue of care

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:36.900
and the issue of poverty.

00:51:36.900 --> 00:51:41.310
And that is, and I'll be
following here what Dr. Tedros,

00:51:41.310 --> 00:51:44.960
Director General of the World
Health Organization said,

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:48.290
which is I then share with him the view

00:51:48.290 --> 00:51:51.350
that perhaps the decisive intervention

00:51:51.350 --> 00:51:54.150
is universal healthcare for everyone.

00:51:54.150 --> 00:51:57.360
I think that addresses
the issue of poverty

00:51:57.360 --> 00:52:02.360
and it directly addresses the
problem of widespread disease.

00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:10.210
If I could wave my magic
wand and do one thing,

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:13.930
I think that would be the
number one thing that I would do

00:52:13.930 --> 00:52:17.091
that would transform American life

00:52:17.091 --> 00:52:21.103
and the safety of the American people.

00:52:23.090 --> 00:52:24.700
- Thank you.

00:52:24.700 --> 00:52:26.861
That makes total sense

00:52:26.861 --> 00:52:31.840
that making universal access to healthcare

00:52:31.840 --> 00:52:36.840
a basic human right that is
guaranteed by all polities.

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:40.890
- There are various ways to do that

00:52:40.890 --> 00:52:45.890
and so I'm not saying exactly
which way has to be the one

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:51.650
but I think the point is
that everyone in the world

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:56.650
needs to have the human
right to healthcare

00:52:56.920 --> 00:53:01.920
and to free access, universal
access, to that health

00:53:01.920 --> 00:53:05.223
and that it shouldn't be
determined as a matter of profit.

00:53:08.407 --> 00:53:09.809
- Our first question...

00:53:09.809 --> 00:53:10.642
Go ahead, Priya.

00:53:10.642 --> 00:53:12.370
- No, no, go ahead please.

00:53:12.370 --> 00:53:13.203
- Sure.

00:53:13.203 --> 00:53:15.333
Considering this issue of red lining,

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:18.570
concerning that it's close
to a century old now,

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:20.650
what do you see in our future

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:22.493
not just for disease but for equity?

00:53:26.340 --> 00:53:31.070
- I feel that I don't know
what lies in our future.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:33.820
I'm professionally a
forbidden, if you like,

00:53:33.820 --> 00:53:37.790
as a historian to have a crystal ball

00:53:37.790 --> 00:53:40.390
and say what will happen in the future.

00:53:40.390 --> 00:53:43.463
But since you've raised the question

00:53:43.463 --> 00:53:48.463
I will give something of a very,
who knows what will happen?

00:53:48.490 --> 00:53:49.840
That's my first point.

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:51.060
Nobody knows.

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:56.060
And a lot will be determined
about whether as in 1918 to 19,

00:53:57.810 --> 00:54:01.310
we decide we want to put this behind us

00:54:01.310 --> 00:54:04.030
and not think about it anymore.

00:54:04.030 --> 00:54:07.480
And that was the approach then.

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:12.480
My hope is that this has
been such a long lasting,

00:54:12.660 --> 00:54:16.350
and will have been by the time it's over,

00:54:16.350 --> 00:54:21.350
such a cataclysmic, economic
as well as medical crisis

00:54:21.970 --> 00:54:26.970
that hopefully we will
be wise enough to realize

00:54:28.670 --> 00:54:30.720
that this must not happen also

00:54:30.720 --> 00:54:32.600
to our children and grandchildren.

00:54:32.600 --> 00:54:36.070
We must leave a safer world for the future

00:54:36.070 --> 00:54:37.720
that's more sustainable.

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:42.720
And therefore part of that
has to do with confronting

00:54:43.750 --> 00:54:48.750
the great inequalities that
our society is built upon

00:54:49.310 --> 00:54:51.290
that didn't arise naturally

00:54:51.290 --> 00:54:54.380
but were part of a consciously devised

00:54:54.380 --> 00:54:56.404
social engineering project.

00:54:56.404 --> 00:54:58.980
It's not, I think,
which is one of the more

00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:01.631
shocking realizations that comes

00:55:01.631 --> 00:55:05.660
from examining these maps of the 1930s

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:09.970
that what they created is still
the way that we live today.

00:55:09.970 --> 00:55:14.513
And therefore I think that
there really does need to be

00:55:14.513 --> 00:55:16.752
maybe there's some talk.

00:55:16.752 --> 00:55:21.690
Myself I'm a bit of, here
I'll reveal my own beliefs,

00:55:21.690 --> 00:55:26.310
but I guess I believe in some
sort of form of green New Deal

00:55:26.310 --> 00:55:30.247
in which we tackle environmental issues

00:55:30.247 --> 00:55:33.930
that are critical for the
survival of the planet

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:36.130
and for the survival of our country

00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:38.370
and the disadvantaged in it

00:55:38.370 --> 00:55:41.414
and also provide employment

00:55:41.414 --> 00:55:46.300
and all the rest for
everyone in the country

00:55:46.300 --> 00:55:48.400
and devising new ways.

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:50.830
I would like to see that happen.

00:55:50.830 --> 00:55:54.745
I suppose if I were
allowed a second magic wand

00:55:54.745 --> 00:55:59.240
that would probably be
the thing I do second.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:56:03.669
And we know that sometimes
genies give you three wishes.

00:56:03.669 --> 00:56:06.800
But my genie first gave me just one

00:56:06.800 --> 00:56:08.170
and you've given me a second.

00:56:08.170 --> 00:56:09.003
So that's good.

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:11.590
- Thank you.

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:13.665
Another participant has mentioned

00:56:13.665 --> 00:56:16.630
the work of cartographers
on the red lining

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:18.536
that you've been discussing.

00:56:18.536 --> 00:56:20.350
He knows that there's a session,

00:56:20.350 --> 00:56:22.349
I guess a zoom session tomorrow

00:56:22.349 --> 00:56:24.946
by the Chicago Maps Society
on the very subject.

00:56:24.946 --> 00:56:28.360
And I think, as I'm
reading this correctly,

00:56:28.360 --> 00:56:30.870
a documentary has been
produced called red lined

00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:33.390
especially on this topic.

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:36.160
- Let me thank the person
who suggested that.

00:56:36.160 --> 00:56:38.160
I would love to see it.

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:42.668
And I'd like to know more
about the cartography involved

00:56:42.668 --> 00:56:43.501
and who the people involved were

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:46.720
who actually drew up the maps.

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:48.470
I know who commissioned them

00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:51.920
but I don't know who
actually produced them.

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:53.602
- Okay, great.

00:56:53.602 --> 00:56:56.560
Just getting another question here.

00:56:56.560 --> 00:56:59.246
- I think there's a question from someone

00:56:59.246 --> 00:57:01.922
who thanks you for the amazing talk

00:57:01.922 --> 00:57:05.720
and asks that she read somewhere

00:57:05.720 --> 00:57:07.970
that San Francisco was
somewhat better prepared

00:57:07.970 --> 00:57:09.530
than the rest of the country for COVID

00:57:09.530 --> 00:57:12.383
because of their experience
with AIDS in the 1980s.

00:57:13.221 --> 00:57:16.126
And she just wanted to ask you,

00:57:16.126 --> 00:57:18.177
is that really true

00:57:18.177 --> 00:57:22.670
and whether the ghost of AIDS

00:57:22.670 --> 00:57:25.173
is haunting the COVID crisis in some way.

00:57:26.888 --> 00:57:29.544
- I would say that there
are a couple of answers

00:57:29.544 --> 00:57:31.270
to that question.

00:57:31.270 --> 00:57:35.970
One of which is that I don't really know

00:57:35.970 --> 00:57:39.980
but from what bits and pieces I do know,

00:57:39.980 --> 00:57:42.200
I would say there's more than one piece

00:57:42.200 --> 00:57:44.660
to that particular puzzle.

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:49.660
One reason is that in San
Francisco there were...

00:57:50.661 --> 00:57:54.890
Another major driver
of disadvantaged people

00:57:54.890 --> 00:57:57.620
in this pandemic has been the elderly

00:57:57.620 --> 00:58:01.040
and the elderly in nursing
homes in particular

00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:02.640
and the disease.

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:07.240
I would say that ageism
is also systematically

00:58:07.240 --> 00:58:09.972
and structurally built into our system

00:58:09.972 --> 00:58:12.610
and that the nursing homes

00:58:12.610 --> 00:58:16.370
as the New England Journal
of Medicine piece there

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:18.420
just in the last few weeks said

00:58:18.420 --> 00:58:20.465
that they were like Tinder boxes

00:58:20.465 --> 00:58:23.350
waiting for spark to be struck.

00:58:23.350 --> 00:58:26.350
That there were systematically unprepared

00:58:26.350 --> 00:58:28.845
and indeed unpreparable.

00:58:28.845 --> 00:58:32.740
The point is that San Francisco

00:58:32.740 --> 00:58:35.567
had a number of nursing homes

00:58:35.567 --> 00:58:38.390
that were radically different

00:58:38.390 --> 00:58:43.390
that we're part of the imagination
of a greenhouse project

00:58:44.913 --> 00:58:49.913
which has small nursing care centers

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:55.030
of perhaps 10 members
residents of a community

00:58:55.030 --> 00:58:59.138
with nurses who actually live on site

00:58:59.138 --> 00:59:02.610
and therefore are not going back and forth

00:59:02.610 --> 00:59:07.510
inside the community, don't
have multiple jobs and so on.

00:59:07.510 --> 00:59:10.260
And therefore the kind of care

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:13.760
and the oversight
practiced in San Francisco

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:17.590
were quite exceptional
in the American context.

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:21.120
I do believe that it is true

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:23.780
that a number of places
by having experienced...

00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:26.880
And this is true not only of San Francisco

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:28.250
but internationally.

00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:31.560
One of the reasons, for example, in Africa

00:59:31.560 --> 00:59:33.880
and a number of countries there was that

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:38.600
they had been prepared by a
whole series of calamities

00:59:38.600 --> 00:59:42.858
from the past, including
Ebola that we all remember

00:59:42.858 --> 00:59:45.423
and we're in a sense ready to accept

00:59:47.740 --> 00:59:51.247
and expect something like this or a place.

00:59:51.247 --> 00:59:54.570
Like it was important that South Korea

00:59:55.532 --> 00:59:57.820
had had the experience of murms.

00:59:57.820 --> 01:00:01.480
And going back further on San Francisco

01:00:01.480 --> 01:00:04.276
there were still community health centers

01:00:04.276 --> 01:00:09.276
and those do really make
healthcare more accessible

01:00:10.590 --> 01:00:15.300
to disadvantaged
communities than a huge city

01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:19.460
with a huge urban hospital miles away

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:23.550
that takes a person without
a car an hour to reach

01:00:23.550 --> 01:00:26.354
by a long bus ride.

01:00:26.354 --> 01:00:29.510
I think those are features.

01:00:29.510 --> 01:00:31.880
I don't believe that is in any way

01:00:31.880 --> 01:00:33.930
an adequate answer to the question

01:00:33.930 --> 01:00:37.663
but those are some features
that I think are parts of it.

01:00:45.260 --> 01:00:47.870
- While we wait for more questions,

01:00:47.870 --> 01:00:50.400
I was curious whether
you had thoughts about,

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:54.818
now that we do have the
prospect of multiple vaccines,

01:00:54.818 --> 01:00:57.218
the distribution of these vaccines

01:00:57.218 --> 01:00:58.899
and how that is going to be set up

01:00:58.899 --> 01:01:02.750
in terms of access and equity issues.

01:01:02.750 --> 01:01:04.963
It is also kind of front and center.

01:01:07.040 --> 01:01:08.930
- That's absolutely correct.

01:01:08.930 --> 01:01:10.210
And we don't quite know.

01:01:10.210 --> 01:01:15.210
We have the CDC recommendations
of how the various tiers

01:01:18.420 --> 01:01:21.590
that the vaccine
distribution should follow.

01:01:21.590 --> 01:01:25.650
And we know from the CDCs announcement

01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:28.530
that this will be months
and months and months

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:33.530
actually will be required
before it reaches,

01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:38.440
let us say, you and me in the community.

01:01:38.440 --> 01:01:41.956
So that is a very
fundamental and important

01:01:41.956 --> 01:01:45.080
ethical and practical question.

01:01:45.080 --> 01:01:47.300
It seems from every point of view

01:01:47.300 --> 01:01:50.420
that the CDC recommendation is right

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:53.953
that healthcare workers
ought to be protected first.

01:01:53.953 --> 01:01:58.700
We depend on them and in
some ways they're the heroes

01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:01.206
or among the heroes of this pandemic

01:02:01.206 --> 01:02:06.109
and they desperately need to be protected

01:02:06.109 --> 01:02:08.303
while they carry out this work

01:02:08.303 --> 01:02:11.730
that's essential to our whole nation.

01:02:11.730 --> 01:02:15.673
So I accept that as a good first priority.

01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:20.456
- Thinking historically
and with your expertise

01:02:20.456 --> 01:02:24.120
in Italy considered,
in the cities of Europe

01:02:25.370 --> 01:02:27.450
what were the fault lines along which

01:02:27.450 --> 01:02:29.653
such segregation might have occurred?

01:02:31.240 --> 01:02:33.950
- Those fault lines are sometimes

01:02:33.950 --> 01:02:37.690
a little bit similar and reminiscent.

01:02:37.690 --> 01:02:42.690
It is true that African-Asian
communities in Britain

01:02:45.280 --> 01:02:50.280
very disproportionately
experienced severe disease

01:02:51.440 --> 01:02:56.440
and mortality in Britain.

01:02:57.030 --> 01:03:00.968
It's true elsewhere that
immigrant communities

01:03:00.968 --> 01:03:03.630
were quite vulnerable.

01:03:03.630 --> 01:03:05.870
But it's interesting too,

01:03:05.870 --> 01:03:08.430
and this goes back to another feature,

01:03:08.430 --> 01:03:10.900
which is that not everywhere

01:03:10.900 --> 01:03:14.680
was the disease primarily
a matter of poverty.

01:03:14.680 --> 01:03:19.460
And I think if you look at after China,

01:03:19.460 --> 01:03:23.210
the second great, cause
you mentioned Italy,

01:03:23.210 --> 01:03:26.379
the second great epicenter in the world

01:03:26.379 --> 01:03:29.600
was Lombardy in the North of Italy.

01:03:29.600 --> 01:03:31.973
And what's interesting about Lombardy

01:03:31.973 --> 01:03:36.973
is that it was the most
prosperous region of Italy

01:03:38.970 --> 01:03:42.370
and one of the great prosperous
regions of the world.

01:03:42.370 --> 01:03:44.971
But there, I would say,
it was overwhelmingly

01:03:44.971 --> 01:03:46.996
a disease of globalization

01:03:46.996 --> 01:03:51.450
because Lombardy and China had established

01:03:51.450 --> 01:03:55.980
a bilateral agreement in 2016

01:03:55.980 --> 01:03:59.290
that led to enormous investment

01:03:59.290 --> 01:04:02.130
between the two countries,
cultural exchange.

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:07.130
Three and a half million
Chinese tourists visited Milan

01:04:07.255 --> 01:04:11.000
every year after the agreement was signed.

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:14.990
There were Chinese lessons,
there were entrepreneurs,

01:04:14.990 --> 01:04:17.300
politicians went back and forth.

01:04:17.300 --> 01:04:20.030
There were fairs between the two countries

01:04:20.030 --> 01:04:22.400
exhibiting the goods of each country.

01:04:22.400 --> 01:04:27.400
So when the epidemic broke out in China,

01:04:27.610 --> 01:04:31.551
probably the least surprising
thing that could have happened

01:04:31.551 --> 01:04:36.551
was that it would next
be present in Lombardy.

01:04:36.730 --> 01:04:40.885
And there were even six airlines

01:04:40.885 --> 01:04:43.270
that did direct flights between

01:04:43.270 --> 01:04:46.500
the Malpensa Airport in Milan

01:04:46.500 --> 01:04:49.120
and a number of Chinese cities.

01:04:49.120 --> 01:04:53.550
So this movement of people,
goods services, investment

01:04:53.550 --> 01:04:56.068
was constant and overwhelming and meant

01:04:56.068 --> 01:04:59.220
that a disease that was infectious

01:04:59.220 --> 01:05:03.170
and had broken out and Wuhan in one day

01:05:03.170 --> 01:05:06.230
would be in Milan the next.

01:05:06.230 --> 01:05:09.073
And indeed that's rather how it was.

01:05:14.100 --> 01:05:19.100
It followed lines of housing
and multi-family occupancy

01:05:20.230 --> 01:05:24.418
and density rather than
anything corresponding

01:05:24.418 --> 01:05:27.123
to the American ethnicity issue.

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:33.140
- I guess the organization
plays a big role today

01:05:33.140 --> 01:05:35.140
in terms of transmission of disease.

01:05:35.140 --> 01:05:38.117
And it's very much part of it.

01:05:38.117 --> 01:05:42.344
- Yes, in fact if I were
to be asked to define

01:05:42.344 --> 01:05:47.344
what I'd say that disease
reflect particular stages

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:49.620
in the development of our societies

01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:51.430
that we humans make.

01:05:51.430 --> 01:05:53.580
The diseases have no agency.

01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:58.370
We create the pathways that
pathogens find to reach us.

01:05:58.370 --> 01:06:02.177
And I would say about this
is the first great pandemic

01:06:02.177 --> 01:06:04.880
of the era of globalization

01:06:04.880 --> 01:06:07.980
and that if we understand it in that way,

01:06:07.980 --> 01:06:12.690
globalization includes
enormous international trade

01:06:12.690 --> 01:06:16.019
and exchange, tourism, displaced people,

01:06:16.019 --> 01:06:21.019
enormous overpopulation,
mega cities and high density,

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:24.736
and an includes all of
the environmental problems

01:06:24.736 --> 01:06:26.368
that we were talking about.

01:06:26.368 --> 01:06:27.944
Plus one would also,

01:06:27.944 --> 01:06:32.630
that is to say about pollution
and all the rest of it

01:06:32.630 --> 01:06:36.142
but one would also have
to invoke our relationship

01:06:36.142 --> 01:06:39.950
with the other creatures
that inhabit our planet.

01:06:39.950 --> 01:06:43.910
Deforestation on the
destruction of animal habitat

01:06:43.910 --> 01:06:47.650
which if we remember
how this disease began

01:06:47.650 --> 01:06:49.841
it's a zoonotic disease that's spread

01:06:49.841 --> 01:06:52.250
from bats to human beings.

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:54.480
Now there was nothing wrong with the bats.

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:57.760
It was that human beings
were increasingly invading

01:06:57.760 --> 01:06:59.910
and destroying their habitat

01:06:59.910 --> 01:07:03.870
and came into contact with
them in ever-growing measures

01:07:03.870 --> 01:07:08.640
and so there were
continuous spillover events

01:07:08.640 --> 01:07:12.183
and those equal health
Alliance has been doing

01:07:12.183 --> 01:07:16.370
a very interesting work or was
doing very interesting work

01:07:16.370 --> 01:07:19.270
until the NIA to continue it

01:07:19.270 --> 01:07:22.490
was cut off by our president.

01:07:22.490 --> 01:07:25.400
But hopefully they'll
start doing it again.

01:07:25.400 --> 01:07:29.160
And the monitoring of that situation

01:07:29.160 --> 01:07:31.843
is quite an important one.

01:07:34.389 --> 01:07:37.380
Part of the reason China
has played such a role

01:07:37.380 --> 01:07:40.130
is that bats are so numerous there

01:07:40.130 --> 01:07:45.130
and they are the reservoir
for countless viruses

01:07:49.250 --> 01:07:52.960
and they are wonderful at
roosting in vast numbers

01:07:52.960 --> 01:07:56.170
and transmitting the disease
to which they're immune

01:07:56.170 --> 01:07:58.330
from one species or one member

01:07:58.330 --> 01:08:00.830
of a fellow species to another.

01:08:00.830 --> 01:08:03.860
And so bats are really very important.

01:08:03.860 --> 01:08:05.850
And then of course China was important

01:08:05.850 --> 01:08:10.850
because of its density
and those sorts of issues.

01:08:12.160 --> 01:08:13.676
There were lots of other reasons.

01:08:13.676 --> 01:08:16.541
Pollution is also very important there

01:08:16.541 --> 01:08:19.740
and mega cities are important

01:08:19.740 --> 01:08:23.270
and even the wet markets
are important there.

01:08:23.270 --> 01:08:25.053
So there are a host of reasons

01:08:25.053 --> 01:08:30.053
that China happened to be the
place that was first struck

01:08:30.170 --> 01:08:32.990
none of which are equivalent to saying

01:08:32.990 --> 01:08:34.690
it was the Chinese disease.

01:08:34.690 --> 01:08:37.290
There was nothing about Chinese people.

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:40.960
It was about certain developmental

01:08:40.960 --> 01:08:44.750
and ecological features
of the Chinese environment

01:08:44.750 --> 01:08:46.734
that were important.

01:08:46.734 --> 01:08:47.567
- Thank you.

01:08:47.567 --> 01:08:49.290
Two more questions.

01:08:49.290 --> 01:08:51.870
There have been studies that
show that some communities

01:08:51.870 --> 01:08:53.270
that have higher mortality

01:08:53.270 --> 01:08:55.689
are actually genetically predisposed

01:08:55.689 --> 01:08:57.520
to be vulnerable to the virus.

01:08:57.520 --> 01:09:00.433
Shouldn't these also be
considered a priority?

01:09:02.420 --> 01:09:05.799
- To the extent to which
that is actually so

01:09:05.799 --> 01:09:07.720
they clearly should be.

01:09:07.720 --> 01:09:11.290
And here I'll have to say that
I myself don't know enough

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:14.920
about genetic predisposition to COVID-19

01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:16.880
to say anything sensible about it.

01:09:16.880 --> 01:09:19.596
So it would probably be better if I say

01:09:19.596 --> 01:09:23.550
if I can look into that,
learn more about it,

01:09:23.550 --> 01:09:25.560
and then I can give you an answer.

01:09:25.560 --> 01:09:28.663
But I better not do that right now.

01:09:31.120 --> 01:09:32.337
- From Professor Valerie Hanson

01:09:32.337 --> 01:09:37.337
who started our series this semester.

01:09:37.520 --> 01:09:39.360
She says, Frank, are there any instances

01:09:39.360 --> 01:09:41.920
either in the past or with COVID

01:09:41.920 --> 01:09:45.450
in which the disease
strikes in surprising ways?

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:48.550
For example, were any
rich communities hit hard

01:09:48.550 --> 01:09:51.450
or did any localities
with non dense housing

01:09:51.450 --> 01:09:52.933
still suffer greatly?

01:09:54.150 --> 01:09:58.470
- Yes, there are these instances.

01:09:58.470 --> 01:10:03.053
First thing is not this epidemic
diseases are by no means

01:10:03.053 --> 01:10:05.750
a simple repetition of the same thing

01:10:05.750 --> 01:10:07.340
happening again and again.

01:10:07.340 --> 01:10:09.190
Each is entirely different.

01:10:09.190 --> 01:10:10.960
So there are diseases

01:10:10.960 --> 01:10:14.670
that have particularly targeted the poor.

01:10:14.670 --> 01:10:17.110
Cholera is clearly one.

01:10:17.110 --> 01:10:19.712
On the other hand the Spanish influenza

01:10:19.712 --> 01:10:22.966
is probably the opposite of the disease

01:10:22.966 --> 01:10:25.650
that struck every social class

01:10:25.650 --> 01:10:29.210
that didn't have this
particular predisposition

01:10:29.210 --> 01:10:32.870
but swept through entire communities.

01:10:32.870 --> 01:10:37.340
And I would even venture
to say that COVID-19

01:10:37.340 --> 01:10:40.330
can manifest itself in different ways

01:10:40.330 --> 01:10:43.960
and Lombardy was a case
in which it affected

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:46.053
a very prosperous community

01:10:46.053 --> 01:10:48.819
and not a very impoverished one.

01:10:48.819 --> 01:10:52.050
So that's a clear example of that.

01:10:52.050 --> 01:10:55.464
And we also have cases where COVID-19

01:10:55.464 --> 01:11:00.464
began by striking in rural
communities and the Netherlands

01:11:02.091 --> 01:11:06.630
and the Eastern agricultural
areas of the Netherlands

01:11:06.630 --> 01:11:08.180
are an example of that.

01:11:08.180 --> 01:11:11.580
And there have been Dutch studies of that.

01:11:11.580 --> 01:11:14.140
And one of the features has to do

01:11:14.140 --> 01:11:17.970
with the overwhelming air pollution.

01:11:17.970 --> 01:11:19.350
- Let me ask one question to you.

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:22.010
- I was just going to
say that there is an area

01:11:22.010 --> 01:11:27.010
of the Netherlands as a highly developed

01:11:27.760 --> 01:11:32.580
industrialized agriculture
produces enormous amounts

01:11:32.580 --> 01:11:36.563
of animal waste and nitrogen oxides.

01:11:37.730 --> 01:11:40.069
So that's one of the features

01:11:40.069 --> 01:11:43.029
that prove to be really important

01:11:43.029 --> 01:11:45.860
in the epidemiology of that.

01:11:45.860 --> 01:11:48.210
So it's again a bit different

01:11:48.210 --> 01:11:51.310
from what I've been just talking about.

01:11:51.310 --> 01:11:55.261
But yes, there are varieties
in epidemic disease

01:11:55.261 --> 01:11:59.270
and I think that's part
of that their interests

01:11:59.270 --> 01:12:03.840
that they reveal a lot about history

01:12:03.840 --> 01:12:06.640
and studying them teaches a lot

01:12:06.640 --> 01:12:09.163
about how communities are put together.

01:12:12.070 --> 01:12:14.010
- I think that brings us to the end.

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:15.516
Doesn't it, Ty?

01:12:15.516 --> 01:12:16.373
- I think so, yes.

01:12:16.373 --> 01:12:18.590
- So I just wanted to say, thanks.

01:12:18.590 --> 01:12:22.900
Thank you so much, Ty, for
helping with this as always.

01:12:22.900 --> 01:12:25.160
And thank you so much, Professor Snowden,

01:12:25.160 --> 01:12:29.320
for a wonderful talk about
really important topics

01:12:29.320 --> 01:12:32.306
that we need to think about now.

01:12:32.306 --> 01:12:36.130
And we are delighted
that you took the time

01:12:36.130 --> 01:12:38.880
out of your very busy
schedule to speak to us

01:12:38.880 --> 01:12:43.880
and we look forward to
actually treating you

01:12:44.150 --> 01:12:46.560
and saying a big thank
you in person to you

01:12:46.560 --> 01:12:48.643
with a very nice reception.

01:12:49.865 --> 01:12:52.403
Here's to a virtual reception

01:12:53.910 --> 01:12:58.910
and a promise to provide
you with a real one.

01:12:59.700 --> 01:13:01.773
So let's all take that take time

01:13:01.773 --> 01:13:06.380
to give him a round of applause
with either our clap buttons

01:13:06.380 --> 01:13:10.530
or unmute ourselves and
just clap and say thank you.

01:13:10.530 --> 01:13:11.610
Thank you very much.

01:13:11.610 --> 01:13:15.970
So this also closes our
Franke program events

01:13:15.970 --> 01:13:16.864
for the season.

01:13:16.864 --> 01:13:20.179
So we look forward to
seeing you in the new year.

01:13:20.179 --> 01:13:22.660
Meanwhile, he is wishing everyone

01:13:22.660 --> 01:13:24.933
a very happy, healthy holiday season.

01:13:24.933 --> 01:13:26.094
Bye.
- Thank you

01:13:26.094 --> 01:13:26.927
- Bye, everybody.

01:13:26.927 --> 01:13:27.760
Thank you

